  "provider_query": "English Wikipedia HTTP article: the default TCP port",
  "answer": "The English Wikipedia **HTTP** article states that the default TCP port for unencrypted HTTP is **80** (443 for encrypted HTTPS).\n\nRelevant results:\n\n1. **HTTP – Wikipedia** — TCP/IP connections use well-known ports, “typically port 80 if the connection is unencrypted or port 443 if the connection is encrypted.”  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP\n\n2. **Port (computer networking) – Wikipedia** — “By default, HTTP uses port 80, and HTTPS uses port 443.” Port 80 is listed for Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_(computer_networking)\n\n3. **List of TCP and UDP port numbers – Wikipedia** — Port **80**: Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP); versions 1.x and 2 use TCP (HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_TCP_and_UDP_port_numbers\n\n4. **Port 80 – Wikipedia** (disambiguation) — “TCP port 80, most often used by Hypertext Transfer Protocol.”  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_80\n\n5. **IANA Service Name and Transport Protocol Port Number Registry** — `http` / `www`: port **80**, TCP (and UDP), “World Wide Web HTTP,” reference RFC 9110.  \n   https://www.iana.org/assignments/service-names-port-numbers\n\n6. **Port – MDN Glossary** — “The default port for the HTTP protocol is 80 and the default port for the HTTPS protocol is 443.”  \n   https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Port\n\n7. **Stack Overflow** — Default port for HTTP is 80 (HTTPS 443); the port is omitted from the URL when it is the scheme default.  \n   https://stackoverflow.com/questions/50888270/why-do-we-need-to-specify-a-port-number-while-using-http-protocol\n\n8. **Super User** — Early HTTP (Tim Berners-Lee, 1991) assumed port 80 when unspecified; RFC 1340 (1992) listed `www` as 80/tcp.  \n   https://superuser.com/questions/996828/why-was-port-80-chosen-as-the-default-http-port-and-443-as-the-default-https-por",
